<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dogū</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as dogu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dogū, &quot;earthen figures&quot;, are small clay figurines of people and animals made in the later part of the Jōmon period of prehistoric <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and no longer made in the Yayoi period that followed. Typically 10 to 30 centimetres high, most appear to be female, with big eyes, small waists and wide hips, and many take them to represent goddesses. Some 15,000 to 18,000 are known, from all of Japan except Okinawa and mostly from the east. Their purpose is unknown; some scholars suggest that illnesses were thought to pass into them before they were broken. They are not to be confused with the later haniwa.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dogu.jpg" alt="Dogū" width="330" height="535" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: World Imaging · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dogu_Miyagi_1000_BCE_400_BCE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Middle Jōmon also left highly ornate clay figurines, the <b>dogū</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/jomon-culture">Jōmon culture · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li></ul></section>
